```{r setup, include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
library(lantern)
library(GMMAT)
RAW <- "../../raw"
```


## Overview

This vignette runs the complete Step 1 → 2 → 3 pipeline on real genotype
data from the Jackson Heart Study (JHS) cohort — chromosome 19, 3 313
samples, local ancestry inferred as African (AFR) / European (EUR) — and
demonstrates `ancestry_smmat()` with all three response types GMMAT
supports: binary (logistic), continuous (linear), and count (Poisson).
Only the `family` argument (and the simulated phenotype) changes between
them; everything else about the call is identical, which is the point —
swap those two inputs and the same code handles your own binary,
continuous, or count phenotype.

* For matrix-level intuition on the two splitting methods, see
  `vignette("split-intuition")`.
* For building `ancestry_split()` inputs from your own VCF + MSP files
  (including K > 2 populations), see `vignette("toy-vcf-msp-example")` —
  this JHS cohort only has AFR/EUR calls, so Steps 1–2 below are shown for
  two populations, but nothing changes for K > 2 beyond the MSP header.

```{r real-step1-2}
# Step 1: split. Step 2: write one GDS per population.
split_real <- ancestry_split(
  vcf_path = file.path(RAW, "LANTERN.phased.bcf"),
  msp_path = file.path(RAW, "LANTERN_chr19.msp.tsv.gz"),
  mode     = "dosage",
  chrom    = "chr19",
  verbose  = TRUE
)
gds_paths <- write_ancestry_gds(split_real, tempfile(), verbose = TRUE)

ids <- split_real$sample_ids
cat("Samples:", length(ids), "\n")
```


## 1  Gene-group file and kinship

The full chr19 gene-group file covers 1 442 genes; to keep this vignette
fast we use a 5-gene subset here (the pattern is identical for the full
file — just point `gene_group_file` at it and expect several minutes per
`SMMAT()` call instead of seconds).

```{r real-genes}
genes_full  <- read.table(file.path(RAW, "genes_chr19_smmat.tsv"), header = FALSE,
                           sep = "\t",
                           col.names = c("gene", "chrom", "pos", "ref", "alt", "weight"))
genes_small <- genes_full[genes_full$gene %in% unique(genes_full$gene)[1:5], ]

kinship <- readRDS(file.path(RAW, "LANTERN_kinship_rds.rds"))
common_ids <- intersect(ids, rownames(kinship))
cat("Genes:", length(unique(genes_small$gene)), "  Common samples:", length(common_ids), "\n")
```

`genes_small` is passed straight to `ancestry_smmat()`'s `gene_group_file`
argument below as a data.frame — no `tempfile()`/`write.table()`/`unlink()`
needed; `ancestry_smmat()` manages the temporary file GMMAT requires
internally and cleans it up automatically. A plain file path still works
too, if that's more convenient for your own pipeline.


## 2  Binary phenotype (`family = binomial()`)

```{r real-binary}
set.seed(1)
pheno_binary <- data.frame(id = common_ids, y = rbinom(length(common_ids), 1, 0.3))

res_binary <- ancestry_smmat(gds_paths, pheno_binary, y ~ 1, kinship, genes_small,
                              family    = binomial(link = "logit"),
                              MAF.range = c(0, 0.05), verbose = FALSE)
print(res_binary$results)
```


## 3  Continuous phenotype (`family = gaussian()`)

Only the `family` and the simulated `y` change:

```{r real-continuous}
pheno_cont <- data.frame(id = common_ids, y = rnorm(length(common_ids)))

res_cont <- ancestry_smmat(gds_paths, pheno_cont, y ~ 1, kinship, genes_small,
                            family    = gaussian(link = "identity"),
                            MAF.range = c(0, 0.05), verbose = FALSE)
print(res_cont$results)
```


## 4  Count phenotype (`family = poisson()`)

```{r real-count}
pheno_count <- data.frame(id = common_ids, y = rpois(length(common_ids), lambda = 3))

res_count <- ancestry_smmat(gds_paths, pheno_count, y ~ 1, kinship, genes_small,
                             family    = poisson(link = "log"),
                             MAF.range = c(0, 0.05), verbose = FALSE)
print(res_count$results)
```

## 5  Adjusting for covariates

Covariates (age, sex, genotype PCs, batch, ...) are just additional terms
in `formula`, as long as the matching columns exist in `pheno` — nothing
else about the call changes. Here we add a simulated `age` and `sex` to
the continuous-phenotype example from Section 3:

```{r real-covariates}
set.seed(2)
age <- round(rnorm(length(common_ids), mean = 55, sd = 12))
sex <- factor(sample(c("F", "M"), length(common_ids), replace = TRUE))
pheno_cov <- data.frame(id = common_ids, y = rnorm(length(common_ids)),
                         age = age, sex = sex)

res_cov <- ancestry_smmat(gds_paths, pheno_cov, y ~ age + sex, kinship, genes_small,
                           family    = gaussian(link = "identity"),
                           MAF.range = c(0, 0.05), verbose = FALSE)
print(res_cov$results)
```

Genotype PCs from your own PCA (e.g. `PC1`, ..., `PC10`) work the same
way: add them as columns to `pheno` and to `formula`
(`y ~ age + sex + PC1 + PC2 + ...`).

## 6  Where to get gene-group files for other species/builds

`ancestry_smmat()`'s `gene_group_file` (a data.frame or a path — both
resolve to the same six columns, no header: `gene chrom pos ref alt
weight`) is fundamentally **your own called variants intersected with
gene boundaries** — it's specific to your VCF, not something you download
ready-made for an arbitrary variant set. `genes_full`, used above, was
built exactly this way: gene coordinates for chr19 intersected against
the JHS variant positions.

What you *can* download are the gene/exon coordinate annotations to
intersect against your own variants (e.g. with `bedtools intersect` or a
GTF-parsing script), for human or any other species:

* [Ensembl BioMart](https://www.ensembl.org/biomart/martview) — gene/exon
  coordinates for hundreds of species (GRCh37/GRCh38 human builds
  included); the classic way to export a custom gene coordinate table.
* [GENCODE](https://www.gencodegenes.org/) — comprehensive human and
  mouse gene annotation (GTF/GFF3), the reference set most rare-variant
  pipelines build their gene definitions from.
* [UCSC Table Browser](https://genome.ucsc.edu/cgi-bin/hgTables) — RefSeq/
  Ensembl/GENCODE gene tables for many species and genome builds, with a
  web UI for filtering/exporting BED files directly.
* [NCBI RefSeq](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/refseq/) — the underlying
  gene/transcript annotation source Ensembl/UCSC also redistribute.
* [ANNOVAR](https://annovar.openbioinformatics.org/en/latest/) — if you
  already annotate variants with ANNOVAR (`refGene`/`ensGene` databases),
  its per-variant gene assignments can be reshaped directly into the
  `gene_group_file` format.

Once you have gene boundaries in hand, build the file per chromosome: for
each variant in your VCF, look up which gene's boundary it falls in
(chromosome + position), write one row per (gene, variant) pair, and set
`weight` (commonly `1` for an unweighted burden test, or a MAF-based
weight — see `MAF.weights.beta` in `?ancestry_smmat`).

## 7  Other ways to adapt this to your own data

* **More than two populations**: nothing above changes structurally —
  `gds_paths` just needs one named entry per population (see
  `vignette("toy-vcf-msp-example")`, Part B, for building the underlying
  `ancestry_split()`/`write_ancestry_gds()` inputs at K > 2).
* **Full gene set / real analysis**: pass `genes_full` instead of the
  5-gene subset as `gene_group_file`, and pass
  `ancestry_counts = split_real$ancestry_counts` /
  `variant_info = split_real$variant_info` (from Step 1) to
  `ancestry_smmat()` to weight the Cauchy combination by each gene's local
  ancestry proportions instead of equal weights.
* **Every `GMMAT::SMMAT()` argument is available**: `MAF.range`,
  `miss.cutoff`, `method`, and `ncores` are named parameters here with the
  same meaning as in a direct `SMMAT()` call; anything else `SMMAT()`
  accepts — `tests`, `rho`, `MAF.weights.beta`, `missing.method`,
  `use.minor.allele`, `auto.flip`, `Garbage.Collection`,
  `group.file.sep`, `meta.file.prefix` — forwards through `...` unchanged.
  See `?ancestry_smmat` and `?GMMAT::SMMAT`.
* **Raw per-population results**: every call above also returns
  `$smmat_results`, a named list of the untouched `GMMAT::SMMAT()`
  data.frames (e.g. `res_binary$smmat_results$AFR`) if you need columns
  beyond the summarized `p_<POP>`/`p_cauchy` in `$results` — burden-only
  p-values, variant counts, allele frequencies, etc.